import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.il0noOp3.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"CAF Govern — enumerated source for the Cloud Governance assessment (Assess: CAF Govern)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"frameworks/cloud-governance-question-set.md","filePath":"frameworks/cloud-governance-question-set.md"}'),n={name:"frameworks/cloud-governance-question-set.md"};function i(d,e,s,c,l,u){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="caf-govern-—-enumerated-source-for-the-cloud-governance-assessment-assess-caf-govern" tabindex="-1">CAF Govern — enumerated source for the Cloud Governance assessment (Assess: CAF Govern) <a class="header-anchor" href="#caf-govern-—-enumerated-source-for-the-cloud-governance-assessment-assess-caf-govern" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;CAF Govern — enumerated source for the Cloud Governance assessment (Assess: CAF Govern)&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>Enumerated 2026-11-24. Verification method and limits are stated below — read them before quoting any coverage number from this page.</strong></p><p><strong>Framework version:</strong> Not versioned by Microsoft — the Cloud Adoption Framework Govern methodology carries no release number. The extraction date above (2026-11-24) is the version, per <code>docs/frameworks/README.md</code>; re-date this line when this file is next re-verified. This replaces the 2026-08-01 enumeration, which was built from the CAF Govern pages as read alongside the retired interactive assessment; every item below is now cited to a CAF Govern page directly.</p><blockquote><p><strong>Source:</strong> <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/govern/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/govern/</a></p></blockquote><p>This is the AB#6811 enumeration for target #8 in the audit&#39;s fourteen-assessment programme (<code>pmo/audits/AZURE-SCOUT-AUDIT.md</code> §14): <em>&quot;Cloud Governance — policy data already collected … Policy compliance state is collected and scored by nothing.&quot;</em> The audit&#39;s §8 Table 3 identifies this as the <strong>CAF Govern methodology</strong> — a different axis from the &quot;Governance&quot; <em>design area</em> that <code>caf.governance.yaml</code> already scores (that design area is part of Ready, covered in <code>pmo/audits/AZURE-SCOUT-AUDIT.md</code> §8 Table 2, not this document). Per DQ12, no <code>caf.govern.yaml</code> (or similarly named methodology file) is written until this enumeration exists.</p><h2 id="why-this-page-was-re-based" tabindex="-1">Why this page was re-based <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-page-was-re-based" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why this page was re-based&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Microsoft deletes its interactive <strong>Cloud Governance</strong> assessment on the Microsoft Assessments platform in <strong>January 2027</strong>. Scout&#39;s earlier enumeration took the five-step process and seven categories from the CAF Govern documentation but described itself as &quot;almost certainly built on&quot; that assessment, whose question text was never published. That framing is retired. Scout&#39;s <em>Assess: Cloud Governance</em> (its <code>Frameworks</code> entry is <strong>CAF: Govern</strong>) is now defined by the CAF Govern methodology itself — the five-step process, the seven risk categories, the example risk register and the example policies Microsoft publishes — and is extended to everything those pages now describe, not only what the retired assessment asked.</p><p>The framework key inside the rule files stays <code>Cloud Governance</code>: the domain-score and report code groups on it, and the assessment keeps its name as an alias.</p><h2 id="what-caf-govern-is" tabindex="-1">What CAF Govern is <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-caf-govern-is" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What CAF Govern is&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Microsoft ships <strong>two</strong> distinct things under &quot;governance,&quot; and this file is about the second one:</p><ol><li><code>caf.governance.yaml</code>&#39;s subject — the Ready methodology&#39;s <strong>Governance design area</strong>, one of the 8 landing-zone design areas, already enumerated and scored (10 recommendations, ~70% coverage per the audit).</li><li><strong>This file&#39;s subject</strong> — the <strong>Govern methodology</strong>, one of CAF&#39;s seven core methodologies (Strategy, Plan, Ready, Adopt, Govern, Secure, Manage). Govern is a <strong>five-step continuous process</strong> — build a governance team, assess cloud risks, document policies, enforce policies, monitor compliance — applied across <strong>seven risk categories</strong>: regulatory compliance, security, cost, operations, data, resource management, AI. Step 1 is done once; steps 2 to 5 repeat.</li></ol><p>Scout&#39;s rules therefore split along both axes. The <strong>seven categories</strong> are the seven scored areas that read Azure (RC, SC, CM, OP, DG, RM, AI). The <strong>process</strong> (steps 1 to 3, plus the audit, review and change-control practices around steps 4 and 5) is an eighth area, <strong>Governance Process</strong> (<code>PROC</code>), that no Azure resource can evidence: every item in it is a manual question, scored when the operator answers it (see <a href="./../assessment/questionnaire-answers.html">Questionnaire Answers</a>) and shown as <em>Not assessed</em> until then.</p><h2 id="verification-method-—-and-the-limits" tabindex="-1">Verification method — and the limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification-method-—-and-the-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification method — and the limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What was read (2026-11-24):</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>What it gave</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/govern/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Build a cloud governance team</a></td><td>Step 1: team function, members, executive sponsor, authority, scope, RACI matrix</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/govern/assess-cloud-risks" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Assess cloud risks</a></td><td>Step 2: identify, analyse, document, communicate, review; the twelve-field risk register and example list</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/govern/document-cloud-governance-policies" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Document cloud governance policies</a></td><td>Step 3: standard policy format and fields, distribution, review and change control; the sixteen example policies RC01 to AI02</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/govern/enforce-cloud-governance-policies" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Enforce cloud governance policies</a></td><td>Step 4: enforcement approach, per-category &quot;Automate … governance&quot; Azure facilitation, manual enforcement, example compliance checklists</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/govern/monitor-cloud-governance" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Monitor cloud compliance</a></td><td>Step 5: per-category &quot;Configure monitoring for … governance&quot; and the cloud governance alerts guidance, remediation plan, regular audits</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Limits.</strong> The <code>CGOV-*</code> identifiers below are <strong>Scout&#39;s own</strong>; Microsoft does not number these items. Microsoft&#39;s example policy IDs (RC01 to AI02) are examples, not requirements, and are cited here only where a Scout rule reads the thing the example describes.</p><p><strong>Shelf life.</strong> CAF Govern is actively maintained content (the AI category and the monitor and enforce pages were all extended recently); re-verify before quoting. The AI content-filtering built-in policies are <strong>preview</strong> definitions: their identifiers can change at general availability.</p><h2 id="the-enumeration" tabindex="-1">The enumeration <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-enumeration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The enumeration&quot;">​</a></h2><p>&quot;Scout can evidence?&quot; uses three marks: ✅ a rule scores it from collected Azure data; ⚠️ a rule scores part of it, or reads evidence but cannot score it; ❌ not observable from Azure, so the item is a manual question. Citations name the CAF Govern page anchor each item comes from.</p><h3 id="rc-—-regulatory-compliance" tabindex="-1">RC — Regulatory compliance <a class="header-anchor" href="#rc-—-regulatory-compliance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;RC — Regulatory compliance&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Citations: <code>monitor-cloud-governance#configure-monitoring-for-regulatory-compliance-governance</code>, <code>enforce-cloud-governance-policies#automate-regulatory-compliance-governance</code>, <code>monitor-cloud-governance#azure-facilitation-configuring-cloud-governance-alerts</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th>Scout can evidence?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CGOV-RC-01</td><td>Policy compliance dashboards are used to get compliance data on assigned policies</td><td>✅ <code>domains.management.policyComplianceStates</code> (needs <code>-IncludePolicyCompliance</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RC-02</td><td>Causes of noncompliance are determined and root-caused, not just observed</td><td>❌ Compliance state is collected; no root-cause field</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RC-03</td><td>A built-in regulatory-compliance initiative (PCI DSS, HIPAA/HITRUST, ISO 27001, NIST, CMMC, FedRAMP, …) is assigned</td><td>✅ Policy assignment whose initiative display name names a recognised standard; a renamed assignment is not recognised</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RC-04</td><td>Alerts are configured for regulatory noncompliance</td><td>❌ Activity-log alert rules are listed as evidence; their condition is not collected</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RC-05</td><td>Sensitive-data compliance is monitored and reported on a defined cadence in Microsoft Purview</td><td>❌ Purview accounts listed as evidence; scans and report cadence are not collected</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="sc-—-security" tabindex="-1">SC — Security <a class="header-anchor" href="#sc-—-security" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;SC — Security&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Citations: <code>monitor-cloud-governance#configure-monitoring-for-security-governance</code>, <code>enforce-cloud-governance-policies#automate-security-governance</code>, <code>monitor-cloud-governance#azure-facilitation-configuring-cloud-governance-alerts</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th>Scout can evidence?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CGOV-SC-01</td><td>Security recommendations are reviewed and secure score is monitored over time</td><td>⚠️ Partial — <code>security.defenderSecureScores</code> and <code>defenderAssessments</code> are point-in-time, not a trend</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-SC-02</td><td>A regulatory-compliance dashboard is checked against common security frameworks (MCSB, CIS, etc.)</td><td>⚠️ Partial — <code>domains.management.policyComplianceStates</code> carries the data; nothing distinguishes which framework initiative each state belongs to</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-SC-03</td><td>Identity governance monitoring is configured — audit/sign-in/provisioning logs, identity secure score, an identity-governance dashboard; MFA and access reviews</td><td>❌ Manual — sign-in/audit logs and access reviews are not collected; the enabled Conditional Access policies that require MFA for all users (<code>domains.identity.conditionalAccess</code>) are attached as evidence</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-SC-04</td><td>Defender for Cloud paid plans are enabled so security alerts are generated</td><td>✅ <code>security.defenderPlans</code> (<code>pricingTier == Standard</code>); not assessed when no plan rows were collected</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-SC-05</td><td>Just-in-time privileged access is used (PIM eligible assignments)</td><td>✅ <code>governance.pimEligibility</code> — the Azure RBAC eligibility schedules, read per subscription; gated on <code>governance.pimEligibilityAvailable</code> (every subscription answered), so an unreadable subscription reads <em>Not assessed</em></td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-SC-06</td><td>Defender security alerts are reviewed, routed to an owner and resolved</td><td>❌ <code>security.defenderAlerts</code> listed as evidence only (rows exist only with <code>-IncludePolicyCompliance</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-SC-07</td><td>A security-standard initiative (MCSB, CIS, NIST SP 800-53, ISO 27001) is assigned</td><td>✅ Policy assignment whose initiative display name names the standard</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="cm-—-cost-management" tabindex="-1">CM — Cost management <a class="header-anchor" href="#cm-—-cost-management" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;CM — Cost management&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Citations: <code>monitor-cloud-governance#configure-monitoring-for-cost-management-governance</code>, <code>enforce-cloud-governance-policies#automate-cost-governance</code>, <code>monitor-cloud-governance#azure-facilitation-configuring-cloud-governance-alerts</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th>Scout can evidence?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CGOV-CM-01</td><td>Cost analysis gives full visibility into cloud costs</td><td>⚠️ Partial — reservations and Advisor score give partial visibility; no full cost-analysis dataset</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-CM-02</td><td>Budgets are created that align with the desired cloud investment</td><td>✅ <code>governance.budgets</code> (subscription scope)</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-CM-03</td><td>Cost-optimization recommendations are used to detect idle resources</td><td>✅ <code>$.advisor[?(@.Category == &#39;Cost&#39;)]</code>, <code>$.costCleanup.orphanedDisks</code>, <code>$.costCleanup.orphanedPips</code></td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-CM-04</td><td>Cost anomalies are identified and a cost anomaly alert is configured</td><td>❌ The alert (a Cost Management scheduled action) is not collected; <code>finops.anomalies</code> is listed as evidence. <code>src/analyze/Get-ScoutCostAnomaly.ps1</code> exists but nothing in <code>Invoke-Collect.ps1</code> calls it</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-CM-05</td><td>Budget alerts are set at the resource-group level (Microsoft example policy CM01)</td><td>❌ Budgets are listed per subscription only, so a resource-group budget cannot be proven absent; any collected resource-group budget row is shown as evidence</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-CM-06</td><td>Every budget has alert notifications configured</td><td>✅ <code>governance.budgets[*].properties.notifications</code></td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-CM-07</td><td>Cost-intensive resource types are blocked by a &quot;Not allowed resource types&quot; policy</td><td>✅ Policy assignment of the built-in definition</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-CM-08</td><td>Tag inheritance for cost allocation is enforced by Azure Policy</td><td>⚠️ The &quot;Inherit a tag&quot; policy route only; the Cost Management tag-inheritance setting is not collected</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="op-—-operations" tabindex="-1">OP — Operations <a class="header-anchor" href="#op-—-operations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;OP — Operations&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Citations: <code>monitor-cloud-governance#configure-monitoring-for-operations-governance</code>, <code>enforce-cloud-governance-policies#automate-operations-governance</code>, <code>monitor-cloud-governance#azure-facilitation-configuring-cloud-governance-alerts</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th>Scout can evidence?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CGOV-OP-01</td><td>Policy compliance is tracked for operations-scoped governance policies</td><td>✅ <code>domains.management.policyComplianceStates</code> (generic — not operations-specific)</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-OP-02</td><td>Logs and metrics are monitored for availability and performance</td><td>⚠️ Partial — <code>management.logAnalyticsWorkspaces</code> existence only, not coverage or content</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-OP-03</td><td>Advisor is used to monitor reliability/security/performance/cost, with alerts set on new recommendations</td><td>❌ An Advisor alert <strong>is</strong> an activity-log alert rule (category <code>Recommendation</code>); Scout collects the rule but not its condition, so it cannot be told from other activity-log alerts. Rows are listed as evidence</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-OP-04</td><td>Service Health and Resource Health alerts are configured</td><td>❌ Same limitation: both are activity-log alert rules whose condition is not collected</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-OP-05</td><td>Activity-log alert rules are enabled and routed to an action group</td><td>✅ <code>monitor.activityLogAlertRules</code> (<code>enabled</code> and <code>actionGroupCount &gt; 0</code>) — the readable half of OP-03/OP-04</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-OP-06</td><td>Alerts are configured on specific logs and metrics</td><td>✅ <code>monitor.metricAlertRules</code>, <code>monitor.scheduledQueryRules</code> (enabled) — existence only</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="dg-—-data" tabindex="-1">DG — Data <a class="header-anchor" href="#dg-—-data" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;DG — Data&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Citations: <code>monitor-cloud-governance#configure-monitoring-for-data-governance</code>, <code>enforce-cloud-governance-policies#automate-data-governance</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th>Scout can evidence?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CGOV-DG-01</td><td>Data compliance, management, and usage are monitored (Microsoft Purview)</td><td>⚠️ <code>domains.analytics.purviewAccounts</code> — a deployed account is necessary but not sufficient evidence</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-DG-02</td><td>Dashboards monitor compliance with data-plane policies</td><td>❌ Data-plane policy state is not collected</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-DG-03</td><td>Encryption policies are assigned (secure transfer, minimum TLS version, customer-managed keys)</td><td>✅ Policy assignment of the built-in definitions, or the Microsoft cloud security benchmark initiative that contains them</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-DG-04</td><td>Encryption in transit: every storage account requires secure transfer</td><td>✅ <code>domains.storage.storageAccounts[*].httpsOnly</code></td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-DG-05</td><td>Encryption in transit: every storage account enforces TLS 1.2 or higher</td><td>✅ <code>domains.storage.storageAccounts[*].minTls</code></td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-DG-06</td><td>Data lifecycle policies are enabled for all sensitive data (Microsoft example policy DG02)</td><td>❌ Storage management policies and Purview retention are not collected</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="rm-—-resource-management" tabindex="-1">RM — Resource management <a class="header-anchor" href="#rm-—-resource-management" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;RM — Resource management&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Citations: <code>monitor-cloud-governance#configure-monitoring-for-resource-management-governance</code>, <code>enforce-cloud-governance-policies#automate-resource-management-governance</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th>Scout can evidence?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CGOV-RM-01</td><td>Policies on resource deployments — e.g. tag-enforcement policies — are monitored for compliance</td><td>⚠️ <code>governance.policyAssignments</code> existence; the specific policies are RM-03 to RM-05</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RM-02</td><td>A resource-management hierarchy exists (management groups beyond the tenant root)</td><td>✅ <code>governance.managementGroups</code></td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RM-03</td><td>Deployments are restricted to approved regions by an &quot;Allowed locations&quot; policy</td><td>✅ Policy assignment of the built-in definitions</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RM-04</td><td>Resource types that add unnecessary risk are restricted by a resource-type policy</td><td>✅ Policy assignment of &quot;Not allowed resource types&quot; or &quot;Allowed resource types&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RM-05</td><td>Tags are enforced by Azure Policy (require, add, append or inherit a tag)</td><td>✅ Policy assignment of the built-in tag definitions</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RM-06</td><td>A tagging and naming strategy is defined and applied</td><td>❌ A document, not a resource property (collected tags are listed as evidence)</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-RM-07</td><td>Infrastructure as code is used to deploy resources (Microsoft example policy RM01)</td><td>❌ <code>management.deployments</code> listed as evidence; source control and pipeline use are not collected</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="ai-—-artificial-intelligence" tabindex="-1">AI — Artificial intelligence <a class="header-anchor" href="#ai-—-artificial-intelligence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AI — Artificial intelligence&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Citations: <code>monitor-cloud-governance#configure-monitoring-for-ai-governance</code>, <code>enforce-cloud-governance-policies#automate-ai-governance</code>, <code>monitor-cloud-governance#azure-facilitation-configuring-cloud-governance-alerts</code> (AI governance alerts).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th>Scout can evidence?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CGOV-AI-01</td><td>AI system outputs are monitored — abuse monitoring and content filtering configured</td><td>❌ Content filters (<code>raiPolicies</code>) and abuse monitoring are not collected; AI-03 scores the readable part</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-AI-02</td><td>Customer-facing AI systems are red-teamed on a recurring cadence</td><td>❌ Organisational/process; no Azure artefact records a red-team cadence</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-AI-03</td><td>AI content filtering is enforced by Azure Policy</td><td>✅ Policy assignment of the (preview) Cognitive Services content-filtering definitions, or a policy named for content filtering. Evidences the policy is assigned, not that deployments are at &quot;medium or higher&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-AI-04</td><td>Microsoft Defender for AI Services is enabled</td><td>✅ <code>security.defenderPlans</code> (plan <code>AI</code>, <code>Standard</code>); not assessed when no plan rows were collected</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="proc-—-the-governance-process-itself" tabindex="-1">PROC — The governance process itself <a class="header-anchor" href="#proc-—-the-governance-process-itself" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;PROC — The governance process itself&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Citations: <code>govern/</code> (step 1), <code>assess-cloud-risks</code> (step 2), <code>document-cloud-governance-policies</code> (step 3), <code>enforce-cloud-governance-policies</code> and <code>monitor-cloud-governance</code> (steps 4 and 5). All eleven are organisational — Scout reads the <em>result</em> of governance being enforced, never the process that produced the policy — so each is a manual question, scored when answered.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th>Scout can evidence?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-01</td><td>A cloud governance team is formally established with a charter, defined membership and cross-functional representation</td><td>❌ Organisational</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-02</td><td>Governance policies are documented in a standard format (policy ID, category, risk ID, statement, purpose, scope, remediation, monitoring tool)</td><td>❌ Organisational — Azure Policy objects carry a display name and description, not this authoring standard</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-03</td><td>Policies are distributed via a centralised repository, with compliance checklists for teams</td><td>❌ Organisational</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-04</td><td>A compliance baseline was established and is tracked against over time</td><td>❌ Scout collects a single point-in-time snapshot per run; no historical baseline is retained between runs</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-05</td><td>Noncompliance remediation has a defined timeline and escalation path scaled to risk severity</td><td>❌ Organisational</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-06</td><td>A named executive sponsor grants the team its authority, and a RACI matrix assigns governance tasks</td><td>❌ Organisational</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-07</td><td>A risk register exists with Microsoft&#39;s twelve fields and is reviewed on a schedule</td><td>❌ Organisational</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-08</td><td>Every governance policy is linked to a risk ID and names the tool that monitors it</td><td>❌ Azure Policy assignments carry no risk ID</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-09</td><td>Governance training is provided to the teams the policies apply to</td><td>❌ Organisational</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-10</td><td>Internal audits (and external audits where required) validate compliance monitoring</td><td>❌ Organisational</td></tr><tr><td>CGOV-PROC-11</td><td>Policy change control: scheduled and event-based review, and a defined way to add, edit or remove policies</td><td>❌ Organisational</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The twelve risk-register fields CGOV-PROC-07 asks about are Microsoft&#39;s: ID, management status, category, description, probability, impact, priority, level (major threat, subrisk, risk driver), management strategy (mitigate, accept, avoid or transfer), management enforcement, owner and closure date.</p><h2 id="microsoft-s-example-policies-and-where-scout-reads-them" tabindex="-1">Microsoft&#39;s example policies and where Scout reads them <a class="header-anchor" href="#microsoft-s-example-policies-and-where-scout-reads-them" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Microsoft&#39;s example policies and where Scout reads them&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Microsoft publishes sixteen example governance policies. They are examples of what an organisation&#39;s policy statements might say, not requirements; this table shows which Scout rule reads the thing each one describes.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>Statement (short)</th><th>Scout rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RC01</td><td>Purview must monitor sensitive data</td><td>CGOV-DG-01, CGOV-RC-05</td></tr><tr><td>RC02</td><td>Daily sensitive-data compliance reports from Purview</td><td>CGOV-RC-05</td></tr><tr><td>SC01</td><td>MFA enabled for all users</td><td>CGOV-SC-03 (manual; the Conditional Access policies that require it are evidence)</td></tr><tr><td>SC02</td><td>Monthly access reviews in ID Governance</td><td>CGOV-SC-03 (manual)</td></tr><tr><td>SC03</td><td>One specified GitHub organisation hosts all code</td><td>Not read — GitHub is outside Scout&#39;s collection</td></tr><tr><td>SC04</td><td>Public libraries go through the quarantine pattern</td><td>Not read — a development practice</td></tr><tr><td>CM01</td><td>Budget alerts at resource-group level</td><td>CGOV-CM-05 (manual), CGOV-CM-02, CGOV-CM-06</td></tr><tr><td>CM02</td><td>Advisor cost recommendations reviewed</td><td>CGOV-CM-03</td></tr><tr><td>OP01</td><td>Production workloads active-passive across regions</td><td>Not read here — covered by the reliability assessments</td></tr><tr><td>OP02</td><td>Mission-critical workloads active-active across regions</td><td>Not read here — covered by the reliability assessments</td></tr><tr><td>DG01</td><td>Encryption in transit and at rest, monitored by Azure Policy</td><td>CGOV-DG-03, CGOV-DG-04, CGOV-DG-05</td></tr><tr><td>DG02</td><td>Data lifecycle policies in Purview</td><td>CGOV-DG-06 (manual)</td></tr><tr><td>RM01</td><td>Bicep used to deploy resources</td><td>CGOV-RM-07 (manual)</td></tr><tr><td>RM02</td><td>Tags enforced by Azure Policy</td><td>CGOV-RM-05</td></tr><tr><td>AI01</td><td>AI content filtering at medium or higher</td><td>CGOV-AI-03 (assigned), CGOV-AI-01 (manual)</td></tr><tr><td>AI02</td><td>Customer-facing AI red-teamed monthly</td><td>CGOV-AI-02 (manual)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="what-this-means-for-the-rule-file" tabindex="-1">What this means for the rule file <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-means-for-the-rule-file" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What this means for the rule file&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>54 items: 26 are scored from data Scout collects, 28 are manual questions.</strong> The 28 manual items are the process (11), plus 17 inside the risk categories where the thing being asked is a human review, a setting in a tenant service Scout does not read, or a rule&#39;s condition that is not collected.</p><p>How the automated rules avoid a false Fail, because absence of evidence is not evidence of absence:</p><ul><li>Rules that read <strong>policy assignments</strong> (RC-03, SC-07, CM-07, CM-08, DG-03, RM-03 to RM-05, AI-03) are gated on <code>governance.policyAssignmentsAvailable</code>: if the policy read failed they report <em>Not assessed</em>. They recognise <strong>direct assignments</strong> by built-in definition identifier (or, for an initiative, by its display name). An assignment made under a custom name, or a definition wrapped inside a custom initiative, is not recognised — the remediation text on each rule says so.</li><li>Rules that read <strong>Defender plans</strong> (SC-04, AI-04) are gated on the plan rows themselves: no rows means <em>Not assessed</em>, never &quot;plans are off&quot;. SC-05 (PIM) is gated on <code>governance.pimEligibilityAvailable</code>, which is always false today.</li><li>Rules that compute a <strong>percentage</strong> (CM-06, DG-04, DG-05) report <em>Unknown</em> when there is nothing to measure a percentage of (no budgets, no storage accounts), never a 0% Fail.</li><li>Rules that read <strong>Monitor</strong> or <strong>Storage</strong> data need those categories in the assessment&#39;s collect (<code>Monitor</code> for OP-05/OP-06, <code>Storage</code> for DG-04/DG-05). A saved collect made without them is refused up front by the collect-provenance check rather than scored as empty.</li></ul><p>The Governance Process area is excluded from the headline score until at least one of its questions is answered, exactly as a manual rule in any other area is. A tenant that answers none of them is not penalised for it; it is simply shown <em>Not assessed</em>.</p><p><code>Management/PolicyComplianceStates</code> remains the single largest lever: RC-01, SC-02 and OP-01 all resolve to it, and it is populated only when the collect is run with <code>-IncludePolicyCompliance</code>.</p>',53)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{h as __pageData,g as default};
